```python
spatial_transitions(
    data,
    variable,
    *,
    w=None,
    k=5,
    m=None,
    per_year=False,
    relative=True,
    title=None,
    gdf=None,
    ref=None,
)
```

Spatial Markov transitions: does a unit's mobility depend on where its neighbours stand?

Rey's spatial Markov chain splits the transition matrix of ``transitions`` by the
class of each unit's neighbours (their weighted mean), and tests whether the
matrices differ. Needs the ``[dynamics]`` extra.

## Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name     | Type        | Description                                                                                                                          | Default    |
|----------|-------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------|
| data     |             | A dataset id, or a panel with ``geo_id`` and ``year``, e.g. from ``load``                                                            | _required_ |
| variable | str         | The numeric column to follow, observed in at least two years; the units without a value in every year are left out                   | _required_ |
| w        |             | Spatial weights from ``weights`` (default: queen contiguity among the units with data)                                               | `None`     |
| k        | int         | Number of quantile classes of the variable                                                                                           | `5`        |
| m        | int \| None | Number of quantile classes of the neighbours' mean (default: ``k``)                                                                  | `None`     |
| per_year | bool        | Classify each year on its own; by default all years are classified together, so classes are fixed levels                             | `False`    |
| relative | bool        | Divide the values by each year's mean first (default), so that classes are levels relative to the average unit; needs positive means | `True`     |
| title    | str \| None | The figure title                                                                                                                     | `None`     |
| gdf      |             | Boundaries to use instead of the project's (a GeoDataFrame with ``geo_id``)                                                          | `None`     |

## Returns {.doc-section .doc-section-returns}

| Name   | Type          | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |
|--------|---------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        | result object | A result with ``.p_global`` (the transition probabilities of all units), ``.p_conditional`` (one matrix per class of the neighbours, from the lowest to the highest), ``.steady_states`` (the long-run distribution in each neighbourhood), ``.fig`` (the conditional matrices side by side), ``.gt`` (the tests of equal dynamics across neighbourhoods), ``.lr_stat``, ``.lr_p``, ``.q_stat``, ``.q_p``, ``.dof``, ``.df`` (one row per unit and year with its ``state`` and ``neighbor_state``) and ``.interpret()``. |

## Example {.doc-section .doc-section-example}

```python
!pip install -q "geo4bolivia[dynamics]"
import geo4bolivia as geo

res = geo.spatial_transitions("ntl", "ln_NTLpc", k=5)
print(res.interpret())
res.fig
```

