::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display .cell-output-markdown}
The spatial Markov chain splits **Log night-time lights per capita**'s 5-state transition matrix by the neighbors' position (spatial lag under queen contiguity (1 island(s) attached to nearest neighbor), row-standardized, n=316), giving one matrix per neighborhood class (5 classes); values were expressed relative to each year's mean first.
The unconditional matrix keeps units in place with average probability 0.829.
Conditioning on context, units surrounded by **low-value neighbors** stay in place with average probability 0.81, while units surrounded by **high-value neighbors** do so with 0.758 — movement is more common where the neighbors have high values.
The homogeneity tests (LR = 112, p = 1.26e-05; Q = 160, p = 6.4e-12; 56 degrees of freedom) are statistically significant at the 1% level: transition dynamics **differ across neighborhood contexts** — a unit's mobility is associated with the state of its neighbors.

_These are associations, not causal effects. A causal reading needs a research design._
